From: locateanything-perf
Subject: the checkpoint's custom code assumes transformers 4.x in five places,
         none of which need the old version

nvidia/LocateAnything-3B: modeling_locateanything.py, modeling_qwen2.py,
batch_utils/hybrid_runtime.py. Apply inside the downloaded model directory:

    patch -p0 -d $LA_MODEL < patches/05-transformers5-compat.patch
    rm -rf "$HF_HOME/modules/transformers_modules"     # REQUIRED, see below

Verified on transformers 5.12.1 / torch 2.11.0+cu130 / RTX 3090, and every
change is guarded so 4.57.1 behaves exactly as it did before.

WHY THIS EXISTS

requirements-model.txt used to pin transformers==4.57.1 with a one-line reason.
The pin was never tested against 5.x; it recorded the first error and stopped.
There are five, they are all small, and none of them is a real dependency on
the old version -- they are places where the checkpoint restates an argument
list or an attribute name that transformers has since moved.

Each is reached only after the previous one is fixed, so they are listed in the
order the traceback finds them.

1. ATTENTION-IMPLEMENTATION HOOK SIGNATURE
   modeling_locateanything.py:74, modeling_qwen2.py:1053

    TypeError: LocateAnythingPreTrainedModel._check_and_adjust_attn_implementation()
               got an unexpected keyword argument 'allow_all_kernels'

   The checkpoint overrides this hook so the string "magi" survives validation
   -- the model ships its own MAGI attention kernel, which transformers does
   not know about and would otherwise reject before the model is built. Both
   overrides copy the base signature of the day and forward it positionally:

     4.57.1   (self, attn_implementation, is_init_check=False)
     5.12.1   (self, attn_implementation, is_init_check=False,
                                          allow_all_kernels=False)

   Neither override reads either flag. Adding allow_all_kernels by name would
   pin it to 5.x exactly as it is now pinned to 4.x, so they forward
   *args/**kwargs -- which is what the _autoset_attn_implementation override
   immediately above each of them already does. The checkpoint's own author
   solved this for the sibling hook and did not carry it across.

2. rope_theta MOVED INTO A DICT
   modeling_qwen2.py:242

    AttributeError: 'Qwen2Config' object has no attribute 'rope_theta'

   Note which Qwen2Config that is. configuration_locateanything.py:9 imports
   transformers' Qwen2Config, not the configuration_qwen2.py sitting in the
   same directory -- so the local one is dead code on this path and the text
   tower is configured by whatever the installed transformers provides. 5.x
   folded rope_theta into a `rope_parameters` dict and turned `rope_scaling`
   into a property aliasing it. Read whichever exists, and say so loudly if
   neither does rather than defaulting to 10000.0 and silently detuning RoPE.

3. _tied_weights_keys BECAME A DICT
   modeling_qwen2.py:1409

    AttributeError: 'list' object has no attribute 'keys'   (in post_init)

    4.57.1   ["lm_head.weight"]
    5.12.1   {"lm_head.weight": "model.embed_tokens.weight"}

   Same tie either way; only the spelling changed. Declared conditionally so
   both versions read what they expect.

4. THE COMPOSITE MODEL NEVER CALLS post_init()
   modeling_locateanything.py, end of __init__

    AttributeError: 'LocateAnythingForConditionalGeneration' object has no
                    attribute 'all_tied_weights_keys'

   raised by _finalize_model_loading, i.e. after all 770 tensors have loaded.
   post_init() is where transformers gathers all_tied_weights_keys, the
   parallel plans and the fp32-keep lists from a composite model's children
   onto the parent. Every stock composite model ends __init__ with it; this one
   never did. Under 4.x nothing read those, so it went unnoticed.

   post_init() also calls init_weights(), which is a no-op here: from_pretrained
   builds the model on meta tensors and loads the real weights afterwards.

5. THE KV CACHE ROUND-TRIPS THROUGH A FORMAT THAT NO LONGER EXISTS
   modeling_qwen2.py:1253/1396/1577-1578, batch_utils/hybrid_runtime.py:1212/1256

    AttributeError: 'DynamicCache' object has no attribute 'to_legacy_cache'

   Both forward passes carry the 4.x BC shim:

     use_legacy_cache = not isinstance(past_key_values, Cache)
     ...  DynamicCache.from_legacy_cache(past_key_values)     on entry
     ...  next_decoder_cache.to_legacy_cache()                on exit

   use_legacy_cache is True on the first forward for no better reason than
   past_key_values being None, so the flag latches and EVERY step of the decode
   loop converts the cache to a tuple and back. 5.x removed the legacy tuple
   format along with both methods.

   In modeling_qwen2.py the fix is simply to keep the Cache object, which is
   what the shim was converting away from.

   batch_utils/hybrid_runtime.py cannot do that, because its caller genuinely
   needs the tuple view: engine_hybrid.py packs rows into a batch, right-aligns
   and pads each row's KV, and slices the result apart again per row, all in
   the (k, v)-per-layer representation. Left as a Cache object it fails one
   frame later, and less legibly:

       File "batch_utils/engine_hybrid.py", line 89, in _unpack_stock_after_forward
         for k, v in out_kv:
       ValueError: too many values to unpack (expected 2)

   So hybrid_runtime.py converts in both directions at that one boundary --
   5.x keeps the tensors on cache.layers[i].keys/.values, and update() on a
   fresh DynamicCache initialises each layer -- and the batch engine is left
   alone.

   prepare_inputs_for_generation reads two more names off the cache that 5.x
   removed: `seen_tokens`, already a deprecated alias of get_seq_length(), and
   get_max_length(), renamed get_max_cache_shape() (still None for
   DynamicCache).

WHY THE CACHE MUST BE CLEARED

trust_remote_code does not execute the files in the model directory. It copies
them to $HF_HOME/modules/transformers_modules/<name>/<hash>/ and imports from
there. Patch the model directory without clearing that copy and nothing you
changed runs -- from_pretrained keeps raising from a path you did not edit.

